    whatIs: "Base64 is a binary-to-text encoding scheme that represents binary data using a set of 64 printable ASCII characters (A-Z, a-z, 0-9, +, and /). It was originally designed to safely transmit binary data over systems that only support text, such as email (MIME encoding) and early HTTP protocols. In modern web development, Base64 encoding appears everywhere: embedding small images directly in HTML or CSS as data URIs to eliminate HTTP requests, encoding authentication credentials in HTTP Basic Auth headers, representing JSON Web Tokens (JWT) that carry user session data between client and server, transmitting binary file uploads over JSON APIs that only accept text, and storing binary blobs in text-only database columns or configuration files. A Base64 encoder takes any input — plain text, binary file data, or raw bytes — and converts it into a string of safe ASCII characters that can pass through any text-based system without corruption. A decoder reverses the process exactly, recovering the original data byte-for-byte. The tradeoff is size: Base64 encoding increases data by approximately 33% because it represents every 3 bytes of input as 4 ASCII characters. This makes it ideal for small assets like icons and thumbnails but wasteful for large files. Understanding Base64 is essential for web developers because debugging authentication failures, parsing JWT tokens, troubleshooting email encoding issues, and optimizing page performance all require knowing when and how Base64 is applied. It is encoding, not encryption — the data is fully readable by anyone who decodes it, so it must never be used to protect sensitive information.",
