    name: "Hash Generator",
    whatIs: "A cryptographic hash function takes input data of any size — a single character, an entire file, or a multi-gigabyte database dump — and produces a fixed-length string of hexadecimal characters called a hash, digest, or checksum. The critical mathematical property of hash functions is that they are one-way: computing the hash of any input is computationally trivial (milliseconds), but reversing the process to recover the original input from its hash is computationally infeasible (billions of years with current hardware). This one-way property makes hashing foundational to modern computing security. Passwords are never stored in plain text in properly designed systems — they are hashed, and the hash is stored instead, so even a database breach reveals no actual passwords. File integrity verification uses hashes to confirm downloads were not corrupted or tampered with: if the computed hash matches the published checksum, the file is identical. Digital signatures, blockchain transactions, git commit identification, and content-addressable storage systems all depend on hash functions. The most common algorithms form a progression of security strength: MD5 produces a 128-bit hash and was once ubiquitous but is now cryptographically broken (researchers can generate collisions deliberately), SHA-1 produces a 160-bit hash and is also deprecated after Google demonstrated a practical collision attack in 2017, SHA-256 produces a 256-bit hash and is the current standard used by Bitcoin, TLS certificates, and most security-critical applications, and SHA-512 produces a 512-bit hash offering the maximum security margin. A hash generator lets you compute all of these digests simultaneously for any text input, compare algorithms side by side, and verify checksums against expected values.",
