    whatIs: "Code minification is the process of removing all unnecessary characters from source code — whitespace, line breaks, comments, optional semicolons, and sometimes shortening variable and function names — without changing the code's behavior or output in any way. The result is a compressed version of the same program that executes identically but transfers over the network significantly faster. Minification directly improves website loading performance: smaller CSS and JavaScript files mean fewer bytes downloaded, faster parsing by the browser engine, reduced memory usage during execution, and improved Core Web Vitals scores (particularly Largest Contentful Paint and First Input Delay) that directly affect both user experience and Google's search ranking algorithm. For production websites, the performance impact is substantial. A well-commented, properly indented 50KB JavaScript file might minify to 15KB — a 70% reduction that translates to noticeably faster page loads on mobile connections where every kilobyte matters. CSS minification typically achieves 30-50% reduction by stripping comments, collapsing whitespace, shortening color values (#ffffff to #fff), and removing unnecessary units (0px to 0). HTML minification removes comments, optional closing tags, and attribute quotes. Modern build tools like Webpack, Vite, esbuild, and Rollup handle minification automatically as part of the production build pipeline — Terser for JavaScript, cssnano or Lightning CSS for stylesheets, html-minifier for markup. However, a standalone browser-based minifier is invaluable for quick one-off compressions when you need to paste minified code into a CMS, email template, third-party widget, or any context where you are working outside a build system.",
